[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next question: the origin of the value. This one decides whether [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is receiving an identity or minting one, and
  they lead to different designs.

  - [1mCaller passes an id it already has[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m with the submit handler supplying it. This is
    literally what you asked for, but the handler has nothing to supply: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m collects only username and
    password, and there's no client state holding an id. We'd have to invent a source, which is the gap that turned this
    from bounded into architectural in the first place.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m derives it from the credentials[0m — the id is the username (or a normalized form of it). Honest about the
    current stub, requires no new input, and "who logged in" is answered exactly. Costs: it's not really a separate id,
    so if a server later returns a real user id, the stored value's meaning changes.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m mints a client-side id per login[0m — e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m, stored alongside the username. Good for "track"
    in the analytics sense: you can correlate activity within a session. Costs: it identifies a session, not a person,
    so it can't be matched to a server-side user record.
  - [1mThe stub returns it, standing in for a server response[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps the parameter as an override, but the id it
    stores is the one the (future) API hands back. This is where a real app ends up, since the server is what knows the
    user's id.

  The parameter you asked for survives in all four; what differs is what fills it when nobody passes one.

  My recommendation is the fourth shaped as the second: keep [38;5;153muserId[39m an optional parameter, have the stub produce the
  username as the id today, and route it through a single module so that when [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m becomes a real call, swapping
  to a server-issued id is a one-line change in one file. That gets you the parameter, gets "who logged in" persisted,
  and doesn't bake a guess about ids into every call site.
